live Marilyn came about after one of the  producers on your last film mentioned he  was editing a book of Marilyn Monroe's  never-before-seen personal papers so  what was it about those documents that  changed your mind about Marilyn because  you weren't a particular fan of hers  before that were you the project came to  me because on my last film Bobby Fischer  against the world  my producer Stanley buck sole mentioned  that he was working with the Strasberg's  on a very interesting book the  Strasberg's had found in a closet two  boxes of Marilyn Monroe's notes letters  diaries poems to-do lists recipes just  like you know a sort of plethora of  various thoughts and ideas that she had  that had never been discovered before  and my producer Stanley buck Saul was  working on publishing those into editing  and putting them into a book when I  heard about this I became very  interested I think as a documentary film  or filmmaker or a journalist you know  when you hear about never-seen-before  documents you're always like wait what  and then you know having them be about  Marilyn Monroe one of the 20th century's  most interesting and well-known people  something that hadn't been seen before  seemed interesting I hadn't known much  about her I knew her as I think most of  us do as a subject of still photographs  as a two-dimensional image of Buda Buda  an icon of femininity but I didn't know  her at all as a woman and in looking at  these documents I began to see that you  know the flesh behind that  two-dimensional image and that actually  she was somebody who was much more  relatable than I think I had ever  expected you know as a working mother of  two and a filmmaker I didn't think that  my life had much in common the things  that I worried about her struggled about  or tried to balance with hers but as it  turns out I was wrong what surprised you  the most about what you found out about  Marilyn from those papers what what I  think one of the things that surprised  me the most was how forward-thinking she  was about her sexuality now of course  she you know Hollywood was famous for  being a casting couch system the studio  system was a casting couch system you  know actresses slept with producers and  you know that's all well known  so it's you know it's questionable  whether to say it's you know feminists  have slept your way to in two parts but  I think one thing that was quite  interesting about Marilyn was her  honesty and her forthrightness about it  it was the 1950s in America as the era  of the gray flannel suit it wasn't a  time that people were talking about sex  and sexuality one great example is when  there were some nude photos of hers that  surfaced and the studio head she was now  becoming a star the studio heads you  know wanted to say oh that's just  somebody who looks like me it's not  really me and she said you know this is  ridiculous  and she went out and she gave an  interview saying yes she took the nude  photos yes they were her and that that  she did it because she was broke and  needed to make money and in such you  know it first of all it's a story that  we see all the time today right and and  the tensions are still very much the  same between you know Studios wanting to  control that and and how you handle it  but the the sort of frankness with what  she discussed it and her decision to do  that despite the pressure I think it  says a lot about her and about the way  she regarded herself and her sexuality  and she was incredibly driven as an  actress wasn't she and what comes across  in the documentary is how much hard work  she put into honing her craft one yeah  another one of the more surprising  things about Marilyn was how hard she  worked at being an actress I think you  know most of us know her again as this  image but we don't think about the way  she actually wanted to be taken  seriously was and and the way that she  what she actually worked on the most was  the craft of acting she became one of  the biggest stars in in the world in  Hollywood playing roles in movies like  gentlemen for blondes and monkey  business of the dumb blonde you know she  was at the height of her career and in  Hollywood and she walked away and she  said you know what I want to study  acting I want to have different kinds of  roles I would like to play dramatic  parts I would like to not just play this  one role which yes I do very very well  but I'd like to do that and other things  too and she leaves Hollywood she breaks  her contract with Fox which was a studio  contract she still had more pictures she  owed them but she walked away from it  and went to study with Lee Strasberg  and Strasburg had a very  big effect on her life some people  question if it was a positive effect but  for her it was this was her mission was  to become a fine actress and to be taken  seriously and this is what you see in  her documents over and over and over  again is her struggle to learn acting to  be taken seriously and to refine that  craft of course the myth of celebrity  and the price that Fame brings whether  it still exists today how do you think  though that things differed in Marilyn's  day yeah I think I walked in thinking oh  it must not have been so intense because  there wasn't TMZ there wasn't Twitter  and texting and you know all the sort of  constant flow of information but you see  with Marilyn Monroe that this culture of  celebrity was incredibly intense perhaps  she was you know there were there were  big female stars before her but I think  that the culture of celebrity you know  crystallized in her and even during her  time the pressure the journalists the  public eye the scrutiny was you know at  a boiling point that was close to  insufferable I think in the first half  of her career and I when I talk about  that I mean like sort of pre 1955 pre  her moved to New York she relished it  and you see her in press conferences she  is just a genius with the press she  flirts 